{"source_key":"sha256-f31d9b9721e4cd8cb3be60e6497f259c","record_index":324,"record_hash":"sha256:1cc5bd9bffb20a39ede4d2d0c5f6cdb033ea1c419b901d018b966f3e0cb85cde","timestamp":"2026-07-14T18:53:17.264Z","kind":"message","source_event_kind":"codex:response_item:message","role":"assistant","text":"Short version: the wider codebase still relies on `archived`, but graph traversal does not. The coupling is confined to final workspace projection.\n\n| Area | Reliance |\n|---|---|\n| Graph traversal / lower bound | No reads of `archived` |\n| Workspace projection | Exactly two reads, both in one file |\n| Legacy integration/push/rebase | Several behavioral reads; one writer |\n| Persistence | Widely round-tripped through TOML/DB metadata |\n| Frontend | No direct production reads |\n\n`Graph::from_head()` queues workspace branch refs regardless of archive state at [init/mod.rs]([REDACTED:path]). Frame calculation and stack collection also ignore it.\n\nThe actual coupling happens afterward:\n\n- [`prune_archived_segments()`]([REDACTED:path]) truncates segments and removes empty stacks.\n- [`remove_empty_branches()`]([REDACTED:path]) has a compensating `&& !b.archived` check.\n\nI agree with your boundary: `archived` is legacy lifecycle metadata, not a Git graph fact. It should not decide workspace visibility.\n\nThe smallest correct change is:\n\n1. Remove `ws.prune_archived_segments()` and its helper.\n2. Change the empty-segment metadata check from:\n\n   ```rust\n   b.ref_name == ri.ref_name && !b.archived\n   ```\n\n   to:\n\n   ```rust\n   b.ref_name == ri.ref_name\n   ```\n\n3. Turn [`just_init_with_archived_branches`]([REDACTED:path]) into a stronger regression: toggling any archive bits must leave the workspace projection unchanged.\n\nThat is one production file. Two graph tests and the legacy [`dlib_rs_auto_fix`]([REDACTED:path]) snapshot currently encode archive-driven visibility and would need updating.\n\nThe flag itself cannot be deleted yet. Legacy integration still writes it, and legacy push/rebase/commit-list paths use it—for example [archive integrated heads]([REDACTED:path]), [skip pushing archived heads]([REDACTED:path]), and [omit archived rebase steps]([REDACTED:path]). Those uses can remain at the legacy boundary.\n\nOne important distinction: removing these two checks establishes “archive state cannot affect projection,” but the current workspace still is not literally `HEAD ^ target_sha`:\n\n- Metadata refs advanced beyond `HEAD` are deliberately traversed and surfaced; [`workspace_projection_with_advanced_stack_tip`]([REDACTED:path]) demonstrates that.\n- Projection follows preferred/first-parent stack paths rather than representing the complete revision set.\n- Empty metadata branch shells can be shown despite containing no commits.\n- Lower-bound and integrated-commit pruning contain policy beyond simple revision subtraction.\n\nSo I would make archive-independence the first, small change. Making the entire display a literal `target_sha..HEAD` projection is a separate semantic change with a larger snapshot and product impact.\n\nNo code changed in this audit.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:844-849|note=[used prior graph projection boundary guidance]\nrollout_summaries/2026-07-01T08-57-54-n6e5-review_workspace_expression_simplification.md:22-35|note=[used prior projection consumer and simplification context]\n<[REDACTED:path]>\n<rollout_ids>\n[REDACTED:entropy]\n<[REDACTED:path]>\n<[REDACTED:path]>","tool_name":null,"tool_input":null,"source_record":{"timestamp":"2026-07-14T18:53:17.264Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"[REDACTED:entropy]","role":"assistant","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"[REDACTED:entropy]"},"phase":"final_answer"}}}